/* DMart-only retail lesson. The parent company and group are overlapping reporting scopes. */
(()=>{
 const id='avenue-supermarts',c=window.COMPANIES.find(c=>c.id===id),previous=window.companyPrimer;
 const urls={company:'https://www.dmartindia.com/about-us',ready:'https://www.dmart.in/',results:'https://api.dmartindia.com/corporate/content/file/v1/2/jZtJ4gV9JIDY9CjsdXXbQxhE1777722483/Outcome%20of%20the%20Board%20Meeting%20held%20on%20May%202%2C%202026.pdf',release:'https://api.dmartindia.com/corporate/content/file/v1/2/R2aWBiIpiuD39xgfm4wrqQKc1777723315/Press%20release%20dated%202nd%20May%2C%202026.pdf',investors:'https://www.dmartindia.com/investor-relations/financial-results'};
 const note=(url,label,location='')=>`<p class="source-note"><a href="${url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label} ↗</a>${location?` · ${location}`:''}</p>`;
 c.summary='The retailer behind DMart stores and the group’s DMart Ready online grocery business. Its work is buying, stocking and selling goods efficiently.';
 c.description="DMart is a retailer: it buys goods from suppliers and sells them to shoppers in its stores. Its shelves include groceries, household items and clothing. The group also runs DMart Ready online grocery shopping where available; it has to keep useful stock ready and sell at prices that cover its costs.";
 c.money="DMart records sales when shoppers buy goods from it. It first has to cover what it paid suppliers, then staff, buildings, electricity, storage, delivery and other costs before profit remains. More customers or bigger shopping bills do not guarantee higher profit if the costs rise faster.";
 window.COMPANY_OVERVIEWS[id]={"customers": "People shopping in DMart stores and using DMart Ready where available. The direct paying customer is usually the shopper at checkout, rather than a shop buying stock to resell.", "drivers": "Purchase prices paid to suppliers, goods available on shelves, customer visits, spending per visit and the kinds of goods sold. Staff, buildings, stock that sits unsold and the costs of online delivery also affect profit."};
 c.productSource=urls.company;
 c.products=c.products.map(([name,type])=>[name,name==='DMart Ready'?'Online grocery ordering · fulfilment varies by location':type]);
 c.productNote='Selected retail categories and shopping channels, not a catalogue of products manufactured by Avenue Supermarts. Availability varies by store, location and online service area. Supplier brands, private labels, physical stores and DMart Ready are different relationships and activities. The FY 2025–26 results do not provide separate profits for each shelf category.';
 c.watch=[
  ['Are established stores improving, or is growth mostly from new locations?','Read the company’s store-age definition and reporting period before interpreting existing-store growth.'],
  ['Does competitive pricing still leave enough after all costs?','Follow buying terms, product mix and gross profit through to staff, property and distribution costs.'],
  ['Is stock turning into sales and cash efficiently?','Look at inventory, shortages, waste, supplier payments and the time goods remain unsold.'],
  ['Are new stores becoming productive enough to justify the investment?','Compare store and selling-area growth with sales productivity, maturity and the cost of expansion.'],
  ['Can DMart Ready serve customers at a sustainable cost?','Check service-area changes, fulfilment, delivery and the subsidiary’s actual financial performance.'],
  ['Are you comparing the same scope and cash measures?','Keep standalone and consolidated results separate, and examine cash generation, investment and lease obligations.']
 ];
 window.COMPANY_RESEARCH_GUIDES[id]=[
  'The May 2026 release discusses growth at stores that are two years and older for the March quarter. Check that age threshold, quarter and comparable base; it is not the same as full-year growth for every store. Prices, customer visits, basket size, selling area and product mix can affect the result. New-store sales should not be interpreted as better performance at established locations.',
  'Read the everyday-low-cost/everyday-low-price strategy alongside the financial statements. The amount left after the cost of goods is gross profit; it must still cover wages, property, utilities, logistics and other costs. Mix changes between food, non-food consumer goods and general merchandise can affect the result. Compare the same margin definition and period rather than treating a discount as evidence of good economics.',
  'Inventory is stock still held for sale. Read it with purchases, inventory changes, sales and supplier balances, and compare matched periods. An inventory increase can support new stores, but slow-moving stock, expiry, damage and missing popular items create different problems. Fast checkout collection helps cash flow, but does not remove the need to fund stock and operating costs.',
  'Read store openings, retail business area and sales-productivity commentary together. A new store needs time to mature and may overlap with an existing catchment. Check spending on property and fit-outs, owned versus leased premises, depreciation and lease obligations. Store count alone does not establish the return earned on the money invested.',
  'DMart Ready is operated through Avenue E-Commerce Limited. Read its own accounts and the group’s channel commentary. The May 2026 release described a renewed focus on home delivery and changes to service coverage. Compare the relevant dates and locations; a smaller service footprint is not automatically proof of either success or failure. Do not calculate the subsidiary’s profit by subtracting parent profit from group profit.',
  'Standalone covers the parent company; consolidated covers the group with subsidiaries and consolidation adjustments. These are overlapping views, not businesses to add together. Read operating cash flow alongside capital spending, borrowings, cash balances and lease liabilities. Profit after tax differs from cash available after expanding the store network.'
 ];
 c.risks=[
  ['Price competition squeezes what remains per sale','Other retailers, local shops and online channels compete on price, range and convenience. With limited earnings left from each sale, discounts or higher running costs can have a meaningful effect.'],
  ['Stock is unavailable, slow-moving or wasted','Missing popular goods can lose a basket of purchases. Unsold, expired, damaged or lost stock ties up cash and can reduce profit. Different retail categories need different replenishment and storage.'],
  ['New stores do not earn enough on their cost','Property access, fit-outs, stock and people require spending before a location matures. Weak local demand, overlapping catchments or rising property costs can limit returns.'],
  ['Online convenience costs more than it earns','Picking orders, handling goods, delivery and acquiring customers add costs. Growing online revenue or changing service areas does not by itself establish a profitable channel.'],
  ['Operating and compliance problems disrupt the shopping experience','Food safety, labels, supplier quality, staff availability, systems and property approvals matter to a large retail network. A local problem and a group-wide issue need different assessments.']
 ];
 c.update=['Service coverage is a decision, not just a growth counter','The May 2026 release says DMart Ready rationalised its delivery channels, renewed its focus on home delivery and discontinued operations in a city during the March quarter. The useful question is whether the resulting network serves customers at a sustainable cost. Read the dated coverage decision with the subsidiary’s costs and performance; expanding into more places is not the only way to assess progress.'];
 c.question='If DMart opens more stores and total sales rise, does that prove each store has become more successful?';
 c.answer='No. New locations can add sales while established stores improve, stagnate or lose demand. Compare the company’s defined existing-store measure, sales productivity and store maturity, then consider stock, operating costs and the investment needed. More stores and better returns are different outcomes.';
 c.sources=[['DMart business and retail categories',urls.company],['FY 2025–26 audited results · parent and group',urls.results],['May 2026 company release · strategy, stores and DMart Ready',urls.release],['Official financial-results library',urls.investors],['DMart Ready · current shopping availability',urls.ready]];
 const groups=[
  ['Foods and groceries','Grocery and staples, dairy and frozen products, fruits and vegetables and other food purchases.','These purchases can bring shoppers back regularly, but freshness, expiry, storage and availability need active management.'],
  ['Non-food consumer goods','Home and personal-care items and other everyday household needs.','A familiar soap or hair-oil brand can belong to a supplier. DMart’s role is retailing it: buying, stocking, displaying and selling it efficiently.'],
  ['Other household goods and clothing','Examples include bed and bath products, crockery, footwear, toys and clothing.','Clothing sizes, seasonal demand and discounts to clear unsold goods differ from everyday food shopping. Selling a different balance of goods can change both sales and costs.'],
  ['DMart Ready','Online grocery shopping operated through the group’s Avenue E-Commerce business, where service is available.','The shopper avoids a store trip, but finding goods in stock, packing orders and arranging collection or delivery still need to be paid for. Check current location-specific availability rather than assuming every store serves online orders.']
 ];
 const basics=`<section class="content-section company-primer" id="dmart-basics"><div class="number">START WITH THE SHOPPING TRIP</div><h2>The business is the store and the shopping basket.</h2><p>Imagine a household buying rice, soap, milk and a household item together. DMart earns its sale by having the right range available at an acceptable price. Its work is different from the manufacturer that develops and markets one of the products on the shelf.</p><div class="primer-groups">${groups.map(([name,offering,meaning])=>`<div><h3>${name}</h3><p><strong>What the customer gets:</strong> ${offering}</p><p><strong>What the retailer has to do:</strong> ${meaning}</p></div>`).join('')}</div>${note(urls.release,'Official retail categories, strategy and online-channel commentary','FY 2025–26 release · PDF page 4')}
 <h3>Supplier brands and private labels work differently</h3><p>A supplier-owned brand can be sold by many shops. A private label is an offering sold under a retailer-associated brand. In either case, shelf space, purchasing terms, quality, availability and shopper choice affect the retail business. Selling a product does not automatically mean Avenue Supermarts owns its manufacturer.</p><h3>Why keep prices competitive?</h3><p>DMart describes its approach as <strong>Everyday Low Cost–Everyday Low Price</strong>: buying on competitive terms and using operating and distribution efficiency to support competitive selling prices. For the investor, the question is whether those savings and sales volumes cover the full cost of running and expanding the network.</p>${note(urls.release,'DMart’s explanation of its cost and pricing strategy')}</section>`;
 const specifics=`<section class="content-section company-primer" id="dmart-specifics"><div class="number">HOW THIS RETAILER WORKS</div><h2>Follow the stock from supplier to checkout.</h2><ol class="primer-route"><li><strong>Choose and buy the range</strong><span>Select products, quantities and supplier terms. Buying more than shoppers want can leave money tied up in stock.</span></li><li><strong>Move and display goods</strong><span>Use distribution, store teams and the appropriate storage to keep useful goods available in the right place.</span></li><li><strong>Complete a shopping trip</strong><span>Customers pay for their baskets. Visits, spending per visit, prices and product mix all affect sales.</span></li><li><strong>Restock and pay the bills</strong><span>Pay for stock, staff, premises, utilities, logistics and other needs. What remains must also be considered against the money invested.</span></li></ol><p>This is a simplified retail route. The online business adds finding and packing ordered items, then arranging collection or delivery, and different products and locations can use different arrangements.</p>
 <h3>A gap between buying and selling prices is not the final profit</h3><p>The amount left after the cost of goods is <strong>gross profit</strong>. It still has to cover running the business. Wages, premises, electricity, distribution, damaged goods, depreciation, finance costs and taxes do not disappear because the checkout collected cash.</p><p>Lower prices can encourage visits and bigger baskets. They can also leave less per sale. Efficient purchasing, stock movement and store operations have to support the pricing approach; high sales alone do not establish a good return.</p><div class="primer-examples"><div><h4>More visits or a larger basket?</h4><p>Sales can rise because more customers shop, each spends more, prices change or the range sold changes.</p><span>Read the available operating measures rather than using revenue as a direct count of customers.</span></div><div><h4>More stores or better established stores?</h4><p>A new location can add revenue without changing the performance of older stores.</p><span>Read the company’s existing-store age threshold and period. The reported comparison is not automatically a measure of quantities sold.</span></div><div><h4>More stock or slower stock?</h4><p>Inventory can increase to fill new stores, or because existing goods are not selling as expected.</p><span>Interpret stock with sales, openings, supplier payments and cash flow before deciding whether the increase is useful.</span></div></div>
 <h3>Store productivity and property costs belong together</h3><p><strong>Sales per unit of selling area</strong> describes how much revenue the space generates. A larger store can produce more sales while being less productive per unit of space. A new store’s early results can also differ from a mature location.</p><p>Property access, construction or fit-outs, staff and stock require funding. Owned and leased premises affect costs and cash differently. Read how a location is funded and what it earns, rather than treating store count as a return on investment.</p><h3>Online retail has another layer of work</h3><p>DMart Ready operates through Avenue E-Commerce Limited. Online shopping still needs stock, order picking, handling, systems and fulfilment. Customer convenience and the retailer’s delivery economics are separate questions.</p><p>The parent company’s standalone accounts and the wider consolidated accounts include different scopes. Several subsidiaries and consolidation adjustments can affect the group result. The gap between parent and group profit is not a clean estimate of DMart Ready’s profit or loss.</p>${note(urls.results,'Audited group results: subsidiaries and retail reporting','Consolidated results and notes')}${note(urls.release,'DMart Ready: dated service and delivery strategy')}
 <details class="primer-detail"><summary>Useful words in DMart’s reports</summary><dl class="primer-glossary">${[
 ['Basket size','How much a shopper buys or spends per visit, on the stated basis. It can change with prices as well as the quantity or type of goods chosen.'],
 ['Existing-store growth','Growth for a defined set of established stores. Read the minimum store age, comparison period and exclusions; it is different from growth including new openings.'],
 ['Inventory turnover','A measure of how quickly stock is sold and replaced. The formula and periods matter; selling quickly helps only if popular items remain available and the economics work.'],
 ['Shrinkage and markdowns','Shrinkage is stock lost through damage, theft or similar causes. A markdown is a reduction in selling price, often used to move goods. Both can affect what remains from sales.'],
 ['Store maturity and catchment','Maturity describes how established a location is. Its catchment is the area and shoppers it serves; nearby stores can compete for some of the same demand.'],
 ['Depreciation and leases','Depreciation spreads the accounting cost of assets over their useful lives. Leases create payments and, under the applicable rules, right-of-use assets and lease liabilities. Cash spending and accounting expense happen on different schedules.'],
 ['Standalone and consolidated','Standalone means the parent company’s accounts; consolidated means the group with subsidiaries and relevant adjustments. These are overlapping scopes, not amounts to add together.']
 ].map(([term,meaning])=>`<div><dt>${term}</dt><dd>${meaning}</dd></div>`).join('')}</dl></details><p class="source-note">Business explanations checked 29 October 2026 using the linked FY 2025–26 audited results and company release. Operating questions and simplified retail mechanics are Company Notebook explanations, not findings that every store or online channel earns an attractive return.</p></section>`;
 function numbers(){const f=window.COMPANY_FINANCIALS[id],p=f.retailContext,fmt=n=>new Intl.NumberFormat('en-IN',{minimumFractionDigits:2,maximumFractionDigits:2}).format(n);return `<div class="primer-number-guide"><h3>Connect a large sales total to what remains</h3><p>For every ₹100 of consolidated revenue from operations, approximately <strong>₹${(f.rows[0].profit/f.rows[0].revenue*100).toFixed(2)}</strong> remained as group profit after tax in FY 2025–26. Calculated as ₹${fmt(f.rows[0].profit)} crore ÷ ₹${fmt(f.rows[0].revenue)} crore × 100.</p><p>This is a company-level profit-to-revenue ratio, not a gross retail mark-up, an individual store’s margin, cash available after expansion or a shareholder return. A small change in costs can matter when only a limited amount remains from each sale.</p><details class="primer-detail"><summary>Why standalone and consolidated totals differ</summary><p>The parent’s FY 2025–26 standalone revenue from operations was ₹${fmt(p.parentRevenue)} crore and profit after tax was ₹${fmt(p.parentPat)} crore. The main table shows the wider group, including subsidiaries and consolidation adjustments.</p><p>Do not add these overlapping totals, or call their difference DMart Ready’s result. The group includes more than that subsidiary. Use Avenue E-Commerce’s own accounts to study the online business.</p>${note(f.source,'Official parent and consolidated statements','PDF pages 6 and 14; consolidated subsidiary list and reporting notes')}</details>
 <details class="primer-detail"><summary>What does the reported store-growth comparison measure?</summary><p>The May 2026 release reports ${p.existingStoreGrowth}% growth for stores two years and older during the quarter ended 31 March 2026. This is a specific quarterly store set, not full-year growth for the entire chain or a direct measure of quantity consumed.</p><p>The release also reports ${p.storeCount} stores as of 31 March 2026, explicitly including one store temporarily closed to customers for reconstruction. Store count is therefore not a count of equally productive, fully operating locations.</p>${note(p.source,'Official store-age definition and count qualification','May 2026 release · PDF page 4')}</details><p>The cited audited results report retail trades through offline and online channels with no separate reportable segments. They do not give a separate profit for groceries, apparel or each store. Do not allocate group profit to shelf categories in proportion to sales.</p><p>Next, compare operating cash flow with stock needs, property and fit-out spending, borrowings and lease obligations. A business can report profit while spending more cash to open and develop stores.</p></div>`}
 if(window.PRODUCT_VISUALS)window.PRODUCT_VISUALS[id]=window.PRODUCT_VISUALS[id].map(p=>({...p,url:p.name==='DMart Ready'?urls.ready:urls.company,category:p.name==='DMart Ready'?'Online grocery shopping · location-specific fulfilment':p.category}));
 window.companyPrimer={skipBusiness(company){return company.id===id||!!previous?.skipBusiness(company);},render(company,slot){if(company.id!==id)return previous?.render(company,slot)||'';return slot==='basics'?basics:slot==='specifics'?specifics:slot==='numbers'?numbers():'';},contents(company){return company.id===id?[['dmart-basics','DMart from the basics'],['dmart-specifics','How DMart works']]:previous?.contents(company)||[]}};
})();
